%[text] # Agentic AI Workflow Prerequisite Check
%[text] This hands-on exercise verifies environment setup before starting the workshop exercises. We will confirm your AI agent can see the MCP tools and run one small MATLAB task.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Workshop Contents"}
%%
%[text] ### Step 1: Use the correct working folder 
%[text] Make `Exercise1` your working folder. This is where the prerequisite checks will run.
% Move into the folder for this exercise
scriptFile = matlab.desktop.editor.getActiveFilename;
scriptDir = fileparts(scriptFile);
cd(scriptDir)
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ### 
%[text] ### Step 2: Set Up MATLAB or Simulink to Connect to an Agent
%[text] **Note: This is already configured for this workshop.** 
%[text] To connect your agent to the existing session, run the following command in the MATLAB command window.
%[text]  `>> shareMATLABSession()`
%[text] 
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ### 
%[text] ### Step 3: Open a terminal in MATLAB and start Codex 
%[text] Use the terminal in MATLAB to run command-line interface tools such AI coding agents, git, and docker without leaving the MATLAB desktop. You can download the functionality here: [Terminal in MATLAB](https://github.com/matlab/terminal-in-matlab)
%[text] - To copy and paste in the terminal use your mouse or  Ctrl + Shift + C or Ctrl + Shift + V respectively.
%[text] - To scroll in the terminal use your mouse scroll or  Shift + Page Up / Shift + Page Down \
% Open a Terminal in MATLAB 
myTerminal = terminal(Theme="dark",StartupCommand="codex");
%[text] You will be asked to give Codex access to this folder. 
%[text] **Action:** Press Enter to continue 
%[text] ![](text:image:9fd6)
%[text] **Note: For participants in China, the Agentic AI tools used in this workshop are not currently supported, hence we are unable to provide access to the workshop environment at this time.**
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ### 
%[text] ### Step 4: Check all MCP tools are available to the Agent 
%[text] Now we must confirm your agent can see the MCP server and tools needed for this workshop.
%[text] We first open our agent (Codex) and then we check our configured MCP server  list. It should include the MATLAB MCP server. 
% Check MCP Server available to use
myTerminal.run("/mcp verbose");
pause(1)
myTerminal.run("");
%[text] **Action:**
%[text] 1. Confirm the MATLAB MCP server is connected.
%[text] 2. Confirm 14 MATLAB tools are listed
%[text]     - 5 MATLAB MCP tools including '`check_matlab_code'`, ' `evaluate_matlab_code'`, and  '`run_matlab_file'` .
%[text]     - 9 Simulink MCP tools including, including '`model_overview'`, '`model_read'`, '`model_query_params'`, '`model_edit'`, and '`model_check'`. \
%[text] ![](text:image:8082)
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ### 
%[text] ### Step 5: Test the MATLAB MCP connection
%[text] We will use a MATLAB plotting task to verify that your agent can send code to MATLAB and that MATLAB can return the result.
myTerminal.run("Create and execute a MATLAB function that plots a sine wave in MATLAB " + ...
    "with the title MATLAB Expo Agentic Workshop"); 
pause(1)
myTerminal.run("");
%[text] **Action:** Depending on the path your agent takes, you will be asked to approve particular actions. Use your own evaluation and approve the decisions as you see fit. 
%[text] Example: 
%[text] ![](text:image:3b9a)
%[text] 
